03:23.880 --> 03:47.360
Let me start by giving you a brief introduction of the Samsung Security Framework, NUCS.

03:47.360 --> 03:53.480
NUCS is a huge, complicated whole system security enhancement based on TrustZone.

03:53.480 --> 03:58.600
contains TMA Trash Zone-based Integrity Measurement architecture,

04:00.280 --> 04:05.720
real-time kernel protection, TMA periodic kernel measurement,

04:07.400 --> 04:12.520
SE Linux enhancement, and many other things to prevent privileged escalation

04:12.520 --> 04:19.960
and unauthorized access to the phone's data. Since we are attacking the bootloader,

04:19.960 --> 04:24.040
we will only discuss the most relevant part, secure boot.

04:27.320 --> 04:32.840
The Android boot procedure starts from the primary boot loader, also called PVL.

04:33.400 --> 04:40.680
It will load the second stage boot loader. In Samsung devices, it is called Secure Boot,

04:41.720 --> 04:48.920
also known as S-Boot. Secure Boot will bring up the operating system from the boot image.

04:49.800 --> 04:54.440
Each boot stage will verify the signature before proceeding to the next stage.

04:55.800 --> 04:59.480
The verified key is stored in hardware drilling manufacturing.

05:01.160 --> 05:05.000
Primary bootloader only runs secure boot if the signature is valid.

05:06.280 --> 05:13.000
We cannot change the primary bootloader. Thus, the phone can only boot the official secure boot image.

05:13.000 --> 05:21.580
Next, Secure Boot will launch the boot image or the requiring image if its signature is valid.

05:21.580 --> 05:29.980
If the signature is not valid, Secure Boot will boot the image with an additional action,

05:29.980 --> 05:39.700
which is blowing the next bit. What will happen if the next bit is set?

05:39.700 --> 05:48.140
The provision of Samsung's warranty depends on the Nux bit, also called the warranty bit.

05:49.280 --> 05:54.140
Trying to boot an unofficial boot image will tamper with the Nux bit.

05:55.780 --> 06:00.660
It is a one-time writable memory. There is no way to recover it.

06:01.620 --> 06:06.720
Some services in Trostone cannot be launched if the bit is set,

06:06.720 --> 06:13.720
such as Lux Container, Samsung Pay, and Trusted Key Store.

06:16.720 --> 06:22.720
After the Galaxy S9, Samsung equipped the storage with default settings.

06:22.720 --> 06:28.720
Only after the first time unlocking the screen, the storage gets decrypted.

06:29.720 --> 06:33.720
The encryption key is stored in the TrustLong application.

06:34.600 --> 06:42.200
We cannot decrypt the storage without unlocking the phone, even if we have root privilege.

06:50.920 --> 06:56.440
Other than the storage encryption key, there is lots of critical information

06:56.440 --> 07:01.880
that can only be decrypted by translate. Once the next bit is set,

07:02.760 --> 07:04.920
most of them will no longer work.

