09:02.400 --> 09:06.720
So what if the device is turned off and we don't know the passcode?

09:10.320 --> 09:22.320
In this talk, we are discussing the scenario in which we can exploit the locked phone and keep sensitive data reachable.

09:26.320 --> 09:37.320
In general, Samsung Secure Boot will verify the boot image and boot into kernel after setting up Secure Monitor and initializing hardware.

09:37.320 --> 09:46.320
But, secure boot will enter audit mode if we push the button down and power buttons during the booting process.

09:49.320 --> 09:56.320
The audit mode allows user to flash stock firmware to manually update the phone.

09:56.320 --> 10:02.320
It not only verifies the signature, but also checks the version number.

10:02.320 --> 10:12.020
Audit mode does not allow the user to flash previous versions or unofficial firmware.

10:12.020 --> 10:19.700
This exposes a channel for interacting with secure boot.

10:19.700 --> 10:27.340
In Audit mode, we found several vulnerabilities which can achieve arbitrary co-execution.

10:27.340 --> 10:44.160
The first one can only get code execution on older models, like the Galaxy S8.

10:44.160 --> 10:46.700
Odin request is very simple.

10:46.700 --> 10:49.740
We send an integer as operation code.

10:49.740 --> 10:56.100
The operation code specifies the function we want to use in Odin.

10:56.100 --> 11:02.660
And then, we follow that with another integer, sub-operation, to tell Odin whether we are

11:02.660 --> 11:07.380
going to set, get, or initialize something.

11:07.380 --> 11:15.780
And finally, we can have some argument.

11:15.780 --> 11:22.660
In Odin Flash Image command, the image size is provided by the user, and there is no check

11:22.660 --> 11:25.980
for it.

11:25.980 --> 11:32.620
Even if Odin decides the buffer location by the size, it has an integer overflow bug,

11:32.620 --> 11:39.460
so we can choose which buffer location we want.

11:39.460 --> 11:43.720
What data is stored behind the buffer?

11:43.720 --> 11:51.300
They are the secure boot code segment, VSS, stack and heap.

11:51.300 --> 12:00.300
Due to the lack of size check, we can override everything and control the call segment, stack, and heap.

12:03.300 --> 12:11.300
Since the Galaxy S8, Samsung has tightened the NNU table and set the call segment to read-only.

12:11.300 --> 12:23.300
Fortunately, USB devices have directory memory access, we are not restricted by MNU control.

12:23.300 --> 12:31.300
And next, we must deal with the cache incohesence problem.

12:31.300 --> 12:38.300
Since USB manipulates memory directly, we cannot affect the cache data.

12:38.300 --> 12:48.300
Unfortunately, while receiving data, the CPU keeps tracking the USB event, which is in a while loop.

12:48.300 --> 12:57.300
Obviously, this code is cached by the CPU. Therefore, we cannot affect the code data.

12:57.300 --> 13:05.300
But there are some memory segments which are not set to be cached, and those segments are the heap.

13:08.300 --> 13:13.300
In the wire loop, it will access a heap pointer.

13:13.300 --> 13:19.300
Since the heap is not cached, once we overwrite heap data with all new bytes,

13:19.300 --> 13:25.300
it will access address 0 and trigger data abort.

13:25.300 --> 13:31.300
Once data abort is triggered, it will jump to the arrow handler immediately.

13:31.300 --> 13:37.300
The arrow handler function has never been executed, thus it is not cached.

13:37.300 --> 13:45.820
We can just put jump-slid on the arrow handle, and we put the share code in front of the

13:45.820 --> 13:55.860
code segment to ensure it will not be affected by the CPU cache.

13:55.860 --> 14:01.220
In conclusion, we overflow the memory according to the layout.

14:01.220 --> 14:07.460
then we can achieve arbitrary code execution in SecureBoot.

14:11.460 --> 14:16.020
But we are not able to exploit S9 any later,

14:16.020 --> 14:19.780
because the buffer location is changed to another address,

14:19.780 --> 14:25.220
which is far from the SecureBoot code segment.

14:25.220 --> 14:30.220
we had to figure out a potential exploit path to exploit the newer Marble door.

14:33.220 --> 14:40.220
In S9 and later, Odin changed the buffer location to a big address, which is behind the call segment.

14:42.220 --> 14:48.220
That is because Odin added the parallel download feature to make the transfer speed faster.

14:48.220 --> 14:55.220
To use this feature, Secure Boot needs to boot up another two CPU cores.

14:55.220 --> 15:03.220
If the cores boot fails, Secure Boot will fall back to normal download mode,

15:03.220 --> 15:08.220
which changes the buffer to the original location.

15:08.220 --> 15:15.220
So, we try to find a way to make the CPU boot fails.

15:18.220 --> 15:30.620
In secure boot, the CPU boot function will not boot the call if there are more than three

15:30.620 --> 15:32.620
calls activated.

15:32.620 --> 15:41.300
Thus, if we can boot another call before enter audit mode, we can make the secure boot fall

15:41.300 --> 15:47.660
back to normal download mode.

15:47.660 --> 15:52.140
That is amazing, there is really a way to achieve it.

15:52.140 --> 15:58.080
Secureboot has another mode, which called ur-debug mode.

15:58.080 --> 16:03.500
The mode has a command that can boot another call for testing.

16:03.500 --> 16:10.220
Most importantly, when it's done, it will not decrease the count of booted calls.

16:10.220 --> 16:17.940
We can keep increase the count of booted calls until it exceeds the limit.

16:17.940 --> 16:27.600
And the UART mode has another command, download, can enter into OD mode.

16:27.600 --> 16:35.520
So if we can enter UART mode, we can exploit S10 with the vulnerability.

16:35.520 --> 16:40.600
We found that SecureBoot will track the resistor in the USB cable.

16:40.600 --> 16:48.620
If the resistor matches a specific value, it will enter UART mode.

16:48.620 --> 16:55.260
But even some researchers successfully entered UART mode in Galaxy S7.

16:55.260 --> 17:02.340
The method was no longer working in Galaxy S8 and later, because the cable was changed

17:02.340 --> 17:11.340
to type C. We have tried many other methods, such as making a type C cable connected with

17:11.340 --> 17:20.460
a pull-down and pull-up resistor, or making the type C cable in accessory mode, or trying

17:20.460 --> 17:29.860
communicate with S10 by the vendor-defined message. All of this work all failed. Secure

17:29.860 --> 17:41.440
cannot detect the resistor so six months later we stopped trying and reported the

17:41.440 --> 17:56.800
bug to Samsung on August 2019 and a several days later Samsung got back to

17:56.800 --> 18:03.700
me and told me that this issue was duplicated. This was a real blow to me,

18:03.700 --> 18:09.520
and I changed my research target to other areas.

18:12.000 --> 18:20.400
Several months later, Samsung released the patch. The title is Potential Integer

18:20.400 --> 18:26.520
Overflow. After reading the description, I guess the reporter did not notice that

18:26.520 --> 18:33.120
that the bug can lead to arbitrary co-execution.

18:33.120 --> 18:39.900
The patch description said it fixed the unsigned comparison issue.

18:39.900 --> 18:46.640
But actually, Samsung did not only make the comparison unsigned, but also added a size

18:46.640 --> 18:51.960
check to prevent buffer overflow.

18:51.960 --> 19:01.500
After looking at the patch, I was wondering if there was any way to bypass the track.

19:01.500 --> 19:12.740
Several days later, I found another vulnerability that can bypass the track.

19:12.740 --> 19:19.380
After looking more deeply into the function related to USB receive, I found that the provided

19:19.380 --> 19:27.320
The size is calculated as a line size with a stringy value.

19:27.320 --> 19:33.940
After cross-referencing the list value with all the areas of the system, we found that

19:33.940 --> 19:38.880
it can be changed by Odin request.

19:38.880 --> 19:45.940
According to the debug message, we know it is used to specify the size of data packets.

19:45.940 --> 19:49.940
the value doesn't have any checks.

19:53.940 --> 19:59.940
Therefore, if we set the size of data packet to a larger amount,

19:59.940 --> 20:05.940
we can bypass the check last Samsung edit in October 2019

20:05.940 --> 20:09.940
and exploit the same buffer again.

20:09.940 --> 20:17.940
This time, I reported the bug immediately.

20:17.940 --> 20:26.940
Several months later, Samsung released the patch in January 2020.

20:26.940 --> 20:37.940
They added a check to ensure the size of data packets cannot be larger than 100,000 in HEX.

20:37.940 --> 20:45.940
The patch gave me an idea, and three hours later, I figure out another exploit.

20:45.940 --> 20:49.940
So, let's discuss the third vulnerability.

20:49.940 --> 20:56.940
This time, we can exploit the Galaxy S10.

20:56.940 --> 21:02.940
Odin has another operation command, can flush the partition information table.

21:02.940 --> 21:12.940
The table is small. Let's only allocate a heap buffer to store it instead of using the big buffer in front of core segment.

21:14.940 --> 21:25.940
We cannot directly overflow it, because there is a check to make sure that the size is smaller than the heap buffer to prevent buffer overflow.

21:25.940 --> 21:33.360
Remember the second vulnerability I just mentioned?

21:33.360 --> 21:38.620
We can use the size of peci data to enlarge the receive size.

21:38.620 --> 21:46.840
Although the page added an additional check to the size of peci data, the limit is 6f

21:46.840 --> 21:49.900
in hex.

21:49.900 --> 21:57.820
But the heap buffer size for the partition information table has only 2000 in hex, which

21:57.820 --> 22:04.980
could lead to heap overflow.

22:04.980 --> 22:13.140
This is the pseudocode that shows what happens when Odin tries to receive data.

22:13.140 --> 22:19.060
Looking to the pseudocode, we can see the USB receive will receive the packet data size

22:19.060 --> 22:28.920
variable only if the request size is bigger than 6F in hex, or if the first receive attempt

22:28.920 --> 22:33.340
cannot get enough data.

22:33.340 --> 22:37.700
This is what we need to achieve.

22:37.700 --> 22:46.420
But the USB receive function will keep receiving data until the past size is reached.

22:46.420 --> 22:54.520
Even if we send data in huge intervals, the device will still be waiting for data.

22:54.520 --> 23:05.740
Actually, we found the bug at the very beginning, but we thought it was an unexploitable vulnerability.

23:05.740 --> 23:11.860
We cannot make the USB receive function get insufficient data.

23:11.860 --> 23:19.420
So we wasted our time on vulnerability 1.

23:19.420 --> 23:25.520
But after seeing the patch of vulnerability 2, I was struck by lightning.

23:25.520 --> 23:31.000
I had a light bulb moment.

23:31.000 --> 23:39.920
This time, I came up with an idea, which was to send only a very small amount of data,

23:39.920 --> 23:46.240
and then remove and reinsert the USB cable.

23:46.240 --> 23:52.680
The first USB receive returned in sufficient size, which allowed us to reach the second

23:52.680 --> 23:54.900
USB receive.

23:54.900 --> 24:05.280
This time, it's received with the size of PEGI data, allow us to overflow the heap buffer.

24:05.280 --> 24:09.940
It's turned into a heap overflow challenge.

24:09.940 --> 24:23.220
We use the traditional heap exploitation technique, house of spirit, to achieve arbitrary co-execution.

24:23.220 --> 24:28.920
There is no check for the double linking list for the heap, so we can fake the chunks and

24:28.920 --> 24:37.840
overwrite the heap's metadata to insert our faked chunk into the heap.

24:37.840 --> 24:44.460
Once we can insert the fake chunk into the heap and free it, we can overwrite arbitrary

24:44.460 --> 24:56.040
address. But there is a limitation. The unused bit must not be zero. Since secure

24:56.040 --> 25:03.540
boot is 64-bit, we cannot overwrite the GOT entry function pointers in data

25:03.540 --> 25:12.300
segment. Because the unused bit is in the higher part of the address, which always

25:12.300 --> 25:20.100
be 0 when the data is a pointer.

25:20.100 --> 25:26.300
The only chance is to override the return address on stack, if we can control the first

25:26.300 --> 25:30.540
local variable in the functions.

25:30.540 --> 25:36.260
But there are only three function calls after we free our fake heap chunk.

25:36.260 --> 25:43.460
Luckily, one of those is the Odding command handler.

25:43.460 --> 25:48.160
The Odding command buffer is the first local variable in the handler.

25:48.160 --> 25:55.160
The command buffer is in our control, which means we can fulfill the limitation and set

25:55.160 --> 26:01.440
the unused bit to non-zero.

26:01.440 --> 26:06.620
Now we can execute arbitrary call in Secure Boot.

26:06.620 --> 26:10.780
What's the next step?

26:10.780 --> 26:17.280
Since we overwrite the BSS stack and heap with all neural bytes, it's hard to recover

26:17.280 --> 26:18.280
the data.

26:18.280 --> 26:28.800
Thus, we restart the booting process in Secure Boot to recover this data.

26:28.800 --> 26:37.540
But we only get exception level 1 in non-secure world, some data in TrustZone already initialized.

26:37.540 --> 26:49.680
We cannot init it twice, therefore we must skip the TrustZone related code to keep TrustZone stable.

26:49.680 --> 26:54.840
And we continue the booting process until loading kernel to memory.

26:54.840 --> 27:01.280
In this step, we replace the kernel image with a custom one.

27:01.280 --> 27:05.720
So, this is our exploit step.

27:05.720 --> 27:11.400
First, we set the size of the packet data to a big number.

27:11.400 --> 27:19.840
Second, we send the Odin PIT Flash command to trigger USB data received.

27:19.840 --> 27:27.840
Third, we remove and reinsert the USB cable, trigger the second USB receive.

27:27.840 --> 27:32.840
This time, we overwrite the metadata of heap trunk.

27:32.840 --> 27:40.840
Fourth, we send another Odding command to trigger mainlock and free a heap buffer.

27:40.840 --> 27:48.840
Finally, the free action overwrite the return address on stack, then we can jump to our

27:48.840 --> 27:57.280
The share code will boot custom kernel to get root privilege.

27:57.280 --> 28:02.100
Now we got the exception level 1 in normal world.

28:02.100 --> 28:10.960
The data in secure world is still reachable, since the nut speed is safe.

28:10.960 --> 28:14.980
But we don't have the screen passcode.

28:14.980 --> 28:20.000
The phone is still locked.

28:20.000 --> 28:22.620
