28:33.480 --> 28:38.960
For example, credit card information in Samsung Pay translate.

28:38.960 --> 28:43.600
There are many ways to reach the data.

28:43.600 --> 28:50.680
We can wait for the user to unlock their phone, as shown in the demo video.

28:50.680 --> 28:56.720
Since we are in non-secure kernel mode, we also can destroy the request from non-secure

28:56.720 --> 29:04.960
user mode to secure world.

29:04.960 --> 29:14.220
Furthermore, it exposes an attacking service for us.

29:14.220 --> 29:21.060
Since the last bit is safe, the data stored in secure world still existed.

29:21.060 --> 29:27.460
There are many more vulnerabilities that we can exploit to get into the secure world.

29:27.460 --> 29:35.820
Our team found more than 10 vulnerabilities in 2019 and still find others in 2020.

29:35.820 --> 29:43.100
Outside of our team, there are 10 times more vulnerabilities found by other researchers.

29:43.100 --> 29:51.980
All of these vulnerabilities need at least economic privilege in non-secure world.

29:51.980 --> 29:58.860
For example, we can attack the gatekeeper translate to decrypt the storage.

29:58.860 --> 30:05.720
In May 2019, we reported a vulnerability in gatekeeper translate, which allows us to

30:05.720 --> 30:09.420
